<section id="office"><h2>Garage office, left of the house door</h2>
<p>A finished 6.5×5.5 ft room (~36 sf) on the gym side of the house door. The 3 ft walkway to the door and the basement stair stays clear. There is no exterior window here and no loft. The basement secret office keeps the glass desk and Aeron; this room gets a simple 30×60″ desk, a mesh task chair, and a monitor arm.</p>
<div class="fig2"><figure><a href="img/office-plan.jpg"><img loading="lazy" src="img/office-plan.jpg" alt="Office plan, to scale"></a><figcaption>Office plan, to scale: 6.5×5.5 ft room, door and sidelight to the walkway, 30×60″ desk, 9k mini-split, power and data at the desk</figcaption></figure><figure><a href="img/office-garage-1.jpg"><img loading="lazy" src="img/office-garage-1.jpg" alt="Concept: the office built on the gym side of the house door, walkway kept clear"></a><figcaption>Concept: the office built on the gym side of the house door, walkway kept clear</figcaption></figure></div>
<div class="full"><figure><a href="img/office-garage-2.jpg"><img loading="lazy" src="img/office-garage-2.jpg" alt="Office interior concept"></a><figcaption>Office interior concept: desk, dual monitors, solid-core door with a sidelight, wall-head mini-split. Borrowed light only — no exterior window.</figcaption></figure></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card"><h3>Build-out</h3><ul>
<li><b>Walls:</b> 2×4, mineral-wool batts, 5/8″ drywall. The gym-facing wall gets two layers of 5/8″ drywall with Green Glue or equivalent, for sound isolation.</li>
<li><b>Door:</b> solid-core (or acoustic) door with a sweep and weatherstripping, plus a narrow laminated-glass sidelight for borrowed light.</li>
<li><b>Ceiling:</b> a finished drywall lid at about 8 ft, insulated above, under the sloped garage ceiling (about 10–11 ft at this wall). That lid helps contain gym noise.</li>
<li><b>HVAC:</b> a 9,000 BTU cold-climate wall head, or a second head on the gym 24k multi-zone, independent of the open garage. Condenser on the south wall, off the septic.</li>
<li><b>Power / data:</b> two 20A circuits, outlets at the desk, hardwired Ethernet to the house, a ceiling light, and a CO alarm (required in a closed garage room).</li>
<li><b>Sound:</b> with the door closed, Zoom and calls should be workable. It will not be a recording studio; gym noise will still leak.</li>
</ul></div>
<div class="card"><h3>Cost</h3>
<p>About <b>$4,000 low / $9,000 likely / $16,000 high</b>, including a separate mini-split head and a simple desk setup, not including the gym unit or the garage subpanel. Confirm the house-door swing and the stair half-wall on site before framing.</p>
</div></div></section>

<section id="sauna"><h2>9×16 wood-fired sauna butted to the deck</h2>
<div class="fig2"><figure><a href="img/p-sauna-backyard.jpg"><img loading="lazy" src="img/p-sauna-backyard.jpg" alt="Concept: 9x16 wood-fired skid sauna butted to the east end of the deck"></a><figcaption>Concept on the real backyard photo: the 9×16 wood-fired skid sauna butted to the east end of the deck, door facing the deck, stove and chimney at the far end. The drawings are the source of truth — this photo is a little short on length, and the door angle is approximate.</figcaption></figure><figure class="listing-only"><a href="photos/p59.jpg"><img loading="lazy" src="photos/p59.jpg" alt="Same view today (listing photo)"></a><figcaption>Same view today (listing photo)</figcaption></figure></div>
<div class="fig2" style="margin-top:14px"><figure><a href="img/p-sauna-deck.jpg"><img loading="lazy" src="img/p-sauna-deck.jpg" alt="Closer view of the 9x16 skid sauna butted to the east end of the deck"></a><figcaption>Closer view: the sauna is butted to the deck fascia, with a step straight from the deck into the door. The chimney in this angle is shown a little too close to the deck; it belongs at the far end, as on the elevation.</figcaption></figure><figure class="listing-only"><a href="photos/p02.jpg"><img loading="lazy" src="photos/p02.jpg" alt="Same closer view today (listing photo)"></a><figcaption>Same closer view today (listing photo)</figcaption></figure></div>
<div class="fig2" style="margin-top:14px"><figure><a href="img/site-plan-sauna.jpg"><img loading="lazy" src="img/site-plan-sauna.jpg" alt="Dimensioned site plan: 9x16 wood-fired skid sauna butted to the east end of the deck"></a><figcaption>Dimensioned site plan (concept, to scale): the exact 9′-0″ × 16′-0″ sauna butted to the deck fascia at the hot-tub end, with the 16 ft running along the remaining house wall. Drain field and tank are on the west / garage side. The sketched reserve is approximate — have a pumper mark it before staking.</figcaption></figure><figure><a href="img/sauna-deck-elevation.jpg"><img loading="lazy" src="img/sauna-deck-elevation.jpg" alt="Elevation: covered deck and 9x16 sauna, with chimney height versus the house roof"></a><figcaption>Elevation from the backyard (concept, to scale): sauna butted to the 31′-0″ covered deck, floors matched at about 1′-6″ above grade (tape the deck). Because the house roof is within 10 ft of the flue, the cap is about 15 ft above grade, pending a field tape.</figcaption></figure></div>
<div class="grid" style="margin-top:14px">
<div class="card"><h3>Siting</h3><ul>
<li>Butted to the <b>east fascia of the covered deck</b>, at the hot-tub end, with <b>zero gap</b>. You step from the deck straight into the 32″ outswing door. Align the door with the ~3.5 ft walkway north of the tub. Relocate the cedar privacy screen now at that east edge — it blocks the door.</li>
<li>The 16 ft length runs along the remaining house wall (about 16′-8″ of house past the deck). The 9 ft depth sits in the 12 ft backyard strip, leaving about 3 ft to the house wall. The 10′-6″ roof still clears the house by about 2.25 ft. This avoids a 16 ft projection into the yard.</li>
<li>The permit first-floor plan (sheet A4) shows the deck as <b>31′-0″ wide by about 11 ft deep</b>, on 6×6 posts with 5¼×11⅞ PSL beams and 5/4 wood decking. The included hot tub already sits in the east bay. The sauna has its own gravel-pad foundation — <b>no load on the deck</b>. Flash the joint, including the 6″ roof overhang that will sit over the deck.</li>
<li>Foundation is three 6×6 pressure-treated skids on a compacted gravel pad. This lawn is relatively flat, so piers are not expected unless a tape shows a slope.</li>
<li>Whitewater Township RC-1 setbacks are 15 ft side and 30 ft rear, with a 35 ft height limit. Woodland Trails allows accessory buildings 75 ft back from the road right-of-way. This backyard corner is well behind the road setback; tape the east side lot line before staking.</li>
<li>The drain field and tank are on the <b>west / garage side</b> of the house, clear of this corner. A sketched reserve in the backyard is roughly 15–30 ft off the house and about 4.7 ft north of this sauna — that sketch is approximate, ±10–15 ft. <b>Have a pumper mark the tank, field and reserve first, then stake.</b> Keep the sauna and its pad off the marked field and tank.</li>
</ul></div>
<div class="card"><h3>The pick: the original 9×16 wood-fired skid sauna</h3><ul>
<li>The exact <b>9′-0″ × 16′-0″</b> outdoor skid sauna framing set (not the earlier 8×12 scale-down). Roof with overhangs 17′-0″ × 10′-6″; height to the top of the 2.5:12 shingle shed is 9′-9 7/16″. Board-and-batten siding, 2×6 walls at 16″ with mineral-wool (R-23). Changing room 4′-6″ × 8′-1″ at the deck/door end; hot room 10′-1 ½″ × 8′-1″ (~540 cu ft), seating for about 8–10.</li>
<li>Wood-fired stove, <b>NIPPA WB-21 or equivalent</b>, purchased with the 12″ thru-wall firing extension so it is fired from outside through the far (east) wall, away from the deck and the house. Use the maker’s listed clearances, an FRT 2×6 zone, protected-wall heat shields, cement board, and fiber-cement at the firing door. The stove is about $2,299, or $2,450 with the firing extension, plus a 6″ Class-A chimney kit, a non-combustible hearth pad, heat shields and a spark arrestor — stove and chimney about $3,000–$4,500.</li>
<li>Height budget above the bottom of the skids: 5½″ skid + 5½″ joist + ¾″ subfloor + plates and 91⅝″ studs + rafter/roof ≈ 9′-9 7/16″ at the high side. Finished floor is about 13″ above the skid bottom. <b>Do not deepen the skids or joists</b> without re-checking that roof height. Raise the gravel pad instead so the finished floor matches the deck (about 1′-6″ above grade — tape the deck; the skid bottom then sits about 5″ above grade).</li>
<li>DIY from the original 9×16 framing set: about <b>$12,000–$22,000</b> in materials, plus the stove and chimney. Builder-built: about <b>$28,000–$50,000</b>.</li>
</ul></div>
<div class="card"><h3>Chimney, hearth, clearances, power, and permits</h3><ul>
<li><b>6″ Class-A insulated chimney</b> at the far (east) end, with a support box, roof boot, removable top sections, and a <b>spark arrestor</b> on the cap. Code rule used here: the top is at least <b>3 ft above the sauna roof and 2 ft above anything within 10 ft</b>. The flue is only about 3–4 ft from the house wall, so the house 6/12 roof is within 10 ft and that second rule governs. Plan the cap at about <b>15 ft above grade</b> (~5–6 ft of pipe above the sauna roof), pending a field tape of the eave and the roof within 10 ft of the flue. This replaces an earlier blanket “keep 10 ft from the house and deck” clearance.</li>
<li><b>Non-combustible hearth pad</b> under the stove and at the outside firing door, sized to the stove maker’s instructions, plus the listed heat shields and wall/ceiling clearances. Don’t sit the stove on the wood skids alone.</li>
<li>Keep a clear working strip around the firing door, and don’t store firewood against the sauna wall that has the stove.</li>
<li>No 240-volt circuit is required. A short 120V/20A run for a light and an outlet is optional; it can come from the nearby deck or house circuits.</li>
<li>An accessory-building permit, and a wood-stove / chimney permit, should be confirmed with Whitewater Township and the county. Tell the insurer. Concept only; not engineered or stamped.</li>
</ul></div>
<div class="card"><h3>Buildability versus the original 9×16 set</h3>
<p>The exact 9×16 framing set <b>is buildable</b> at this corner if it is butted to the deck and the 16 ft runs along the house. Specs that fit as drawn, and the few adjustments this site needs:</p>
<ul>
<li><b>Fits:</b> 9′-0″ × 16′-0″ framing, 17′-0″ × 10′-6″ roof, 9′-9 7/16″ height, 2.5:12 shed, three 6×6 PT skids, 32″ outswing door, changing room and hot room sizes, NIPPA WB-21 (or equivalent) with listed clearances, west-side drain field and tank (clear), RC-1 and HOA setbacks at this corner (tape the east side lot line), and a separate gravel-pad foundation on this relatively flat lawn.</li>
<li><b>Adjust — orientation:</b> run the 16 ft along the remaining house wall, not 16 ft out into the yard, so the building stays off the sketched reserve.</li>
<li><b>Adjust — door and floor:</b> door faces the deck (not the yard); raise the pad so the finished floor matches the deck (about 1′-6″ a.g., tape it). Do not deepen the skids or joists.</li>
<li><b>Adjust — chimney:</b> 3 ft above the sauna roof is not enough here; use the 2-ft-above-house-roof rule, cap about 15 ft a.g., pending a tape.</li>
<li><b>Adjust — joint:</b> relocate the east-edge privacy screen; flash the 6″ roof overhang that will sit over the deck; do not ledger or bear any sauna load on the deck framing.</li>
<li><b>Adjust — reserve:</b> the sketched reserve is about 4.7 ft north of the sauna (±10–15 ft). Have a pumper mark the field, tank and reserve first, then stake.</li>
</ul></div></div></section>

<section id="outdoors"><h2>Trails from the back door</h2><p>The west lot line borders state forest. An old two-track from the backyard reaches the Vasa Singletrack in about half a mile, and the Sand Lakes Quiet Area, Muncie Lakes and the main Vasa trailhead are each a short drive.</p>
